- **Tenants never write the shared cache.** They *trigger* a compile; **our** trusted
  Firecracker worker, running our **pinned, version-stamped toolchain**, performs the
  build and **signs** the artifact with the fleet key. There is no
  tenant-supplied-output upload path into the shared pool, so "first-to-cache-wins" is
  impossible — an attacker cannot write at all.
- **Readers verify the signature**, not just the hash. An unsigned or wrong-signed
  artifact is rejected and recompiled.
- **Untrusted CI contexts are scoped.** A PR / draft / preview build writes only to an
  **isolated, context-scoped** cache, never the shared production one — CREEP's
  "hierarchical write-access" mitigation applied to in-nexus CI/CD: protected refs
  write the shared cache, untrusted refs write their own branch-scoped cache.
- **Determinism audit (defense in depth).** Because the toolchain is pinned and wasm
  compiles are deterministic, a background rebuilder re-compiles a sample of shared
  artifacts on an independent worker and compares hashes; a mismatch raises a poisoning
  / non-determinism alarm (the reproducible-builds rebuilder pattern).

## Verifiable provenance — signatures over a transparency log (Radicle-pattern)

The integrity model above trusts *our* fleet signing key. We can do better and make
every artifact and every build **cryptographically verifiable by anyone**, with no
central authority to trust — the [Radicle](https://radicle.dev/) philosophy
(self-certifying, every action signed, trust from cryptography not from where data
lives). Radicle itself is **not** a blockchain, and neither is this: the same
guarantees come from signed records over a hash-linked log, at a fraction of the cost.
Three layers:

- **Signed build attestations (artifact layer).** Every shared-cache artifact carries
  a signed statement — `{source_integrity_hash, lane, toolchain_version+digest,
  builder_pubkey, output_hash}` — signed by the builder key. This is the
  SLSA-provenance / in-toto attestation shape: *Radicle for build outputs.* A reader
  verifies the signature chains to a trusted builder root before accepting the
  artifact. **This is what actually defeats CREEP** — a poisoned artifact has no valid
  attestation from a trusted builder, regardless of matching input hashes.
- **A transparency log, not a blockchain (the record).** An append-only, hash-linked
  (Merkle) log of every attestation: tamper-evident, publicly/tenant-verifiable,
  history-immutable — the guarantees people mean by "on chain," without a chain
  (Certificate-Transparency / Sigstore-Rekor, and effectively how Radicle's signed
  history works). **This log is the same structure as the billing/provenance ledger
  below** — one append-only signed record serves both audit and metering, making
  billing non-repudiable.
- **Capability tokens for invocation (identity).** Each build runs under a signed,
  scoped capability token (biscuit/macaroon-style, extending the existing `wbk_` PAT /
  `Nexus.Auth`); the attestation records *who* invoked it, binding cost to a key rather
  than an IP. You cannot repudiate a build you signed for.

**The store is untrusted by design.** Trust lives in signatures plus content hashes,
so the bucket's location or operator is irrelevant — a swapped or tampered artifact
fails verification no matter who controls the store. The nexus accepts only artifacts
whose attestation is signed by a builder key in its own trust root, so the store can
inject nothing a builder did not authorize. (Distinct surface, same philosophy: the
*repo/git* side — the nexus is already a git remote — is where the Radicle **protocol**
itself fits, via self-certifying signed repos; an interop target as native auth
matures.)

## Usage, billing & abuse — airtight by construction

Billing must be admission-controlled, not just metered after the fact, or a tenant can
run unbilled or runaway compute.

- **Admission control before dispatch.** A compile/CI request checks the tenant's
  remaining allowance (`Nexus.Config` quota) **before** a worker is spawned. At zero:
  queue, degrade to the slow in-process path, or reject — **never** a silent unbilled
  run.
- **Hard per-job caps.** Firecracker enforces a wallclock, memory and CPU ceiling per
  job; a runaway is killed. A single compile cannot exceed its bounded shape.
- **Per-tenant concurrency cap.** At most N concurrent workers per tenant — anti
  fork-bomb.
- **Single-flight dedup.** 100 concurrent requests for the same cache-miss key spawn
  **one** worker, not 100 — both a cost control and an abuse control.
- **Submission rate limit + egress cap** (the CI artifact-egress line).
- **The cache is the primary abuse mitigation.** Hits are ~free; only *misses* cost;
  and misses are capped + single-flighted. The one pathological case — a tenant forcing
  endless *unique* misses (randomized source) — is bounded by the quota backstop and a
  per-tenant miss-rate alarm.
- **Reconciled, idempotent ledger.** Metering is nexus state, reconciled against Fly's
  actual machine-seconds, so we bill *reality* and never drift over or under. The
  ledger is runtime state and the quotas are `.work` `deploy` knobs — no JSON sidecar.

## The fleet — cheapest viable shape

- **Baked compiler machine image, not per-job pull.** The compiler toolchain is
  ~7.2 GB. Pulling it per job dwarfs a 15 s compile (~10×) — fatal. Instead bake it
  into the worker's rootfs once; a **stopped** worker holds it for **$0.15/GB/mo
  (~$1.08/mo)** and **resumes in ~1–5 s** with no pull.
- **Scale to zero, resume on demand.** `auto_stop_machines` + `min_machines_running: 0`
  (already how `Nexus.Provisioner` builds machine config) — a worker costs only its
  stopped rootfs when idle.
- **Right shape per workload.** `shared-cpu-2x` for compiles, `performance-4x` for CI.
  Never one shared size.
- **Pin CI artifact output to object storage**, never push large image layers back out
  through Fly egress ($0.02/GB) — egress can rival compute for image-publishing
  pipelines.
- **Reuse the existing seam.** Workers are provisioned through the same
  `Nexus.Provisioner` / `Nexus.Fly` machine-create path that already provisions tenant
  nexuses — not a second control plane.

## Tier mapping

Meter two units — `compile-jobs` and `ci-minutes` — as `.work` `deploy`-block knobs
read via `Nexus.Config`, neutral defaults in the runtime, our real numbers in our
DeployKit config (the same THE-LINE discipline as `tiers`). Indicative allowances
against our current tiers (Starter $0 / Team $49 / Scale $199):

| Tier | Included compiles/mo | Included CI | Cost to us | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Starter | ~3,000 | — | ~$1.38 | loss-leader; the OOM-relief that makes a 1 GB box usable |
| Team | ~60,000 | ~300 min | ~$7.50 | ~$41 gross headroom |
| Scale | ~600,000 | ~3,000 min | ~$75 | ~$124 gross headroom |

Overage (keep ~3–4× margin): compiles **~$1 / 5,000**, CI **~$0.015/min** (still under
GHA on stronger hardware). These numbers *assume cache misses are the minority* — the
shared cache is what keeps cost-to-us this low.

## The one cost risk

**Idle rootfs × tenant fan-out.** $1.08/mo per stopped baked worker is trivial per
tenant but **linear in tenant count** — 1,000 tenants each holding a 7.2 GB worker is
~$1,080/mo of pure idle storage. Mitigations, in order: (1) **share one compile-worker
app per N tenants** with per-tenant sandboxing rather than one image each; (2)
**garbage-collect** workers idle > X days, eat one cold re-bake on the next compile;
(3) **thin the baked image per-language** — pull only the toolchains a tenant's
declared `languages=` needs. Watch this line as tenant count grows; it is the only cost
that scales without usage. The compile cache makes (1) easy: a shared worker pool is
fine *because* the cache, not the worker, holds the valuable state.

## Build order

1. **Compile cache, shared scope first** — content-addressed key + object store +
   hit/miss in the compile lane. This alone removes most recompiles with **zero** new
   compute infra (misses still compile in-process for now). Biggest win per unit of
   work.
2. **`job` primitive + Fly worker** — baked compiler image, scale-to-zero, dispatched
   on a cache miss. Moves the heavy compile off the serving box.
3. **Private tenant scope + signing** — the security boundary, encrypted-at-rest.
4. **CI/CD jobs** — `flow`-over-`job` wired to `hook`/push/cron triggers; object-store
   artifact output; the two metered units in `Nexus.Config`.

Step 1 is independently valuable and ships first; the fleet (step 2) only pays off once
the cache is deciding what actually needs compiling.
